WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.005
Counting is maximum likelihood.

2
00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:07.491
Write n_ij for the number of times token j follows token i in the training data.

3
00:00:07.771 --> 00:00:15.344
The model’s parameters are the conditional probabilities p_ij, one distribution per row i, with each row summing to 1.

4
00:00:15.624 --> 00:00:20.595
The log-likelihood of the data is the sum over all pairs of n_ij log p_ij.

5
00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:24.416
Maximize it row by row, subject to the row constraint.

6
00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:47.267
With a Lagrange multiplier lambda for row i, setting the derivative of (sum over j of n_ij log p_ij, minus lambda times (sum over j of p_ij minus 1)) to zero gives n_ij / p_ij = lambda, so p_ij is proportional to n_ij, and normalizing gives p_ij = n_ij divided by the row total n_i.

7
00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:51.984
The maximum likelihood estimate is just the row-normalized count matrix.

8
00:00:52.264 --> 00:01:04.808
That’s what the video calls “training the model” at 12:45: counting is the closed-form solution of the same optimization problem that gradient descent solves approximately in the second half.

9
00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:13.323
Having a closed form is rare, and it gives you a precious thing for the rest of the lecture: a known right answer to check the neural network against.
